WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.580
A desert iguana basking on a rock can end up warmer than you are.

2
00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:11.380
So what does cold-blooded mean?

3
00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:18.060
Body temperature could depend on the heat an animal makes, the heat it loses, or a

4
00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:24.560
controller. Metabolic rate is energy used per unit of time, and nearly all of it

5
00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:25.560
ends up as heat.

6
00:00:26.333 --> 00:00:29.113
Mammals and birds are endotherms.

7
00:00:29.813 --> 00:00:34.173
They warm themselves mainly with heat from their own metabolism, held in by

8
00:00:34.173 --> 00:00:39.953
insulation. Ectotherms, like the desert iguana, take their temperature mainly from

9
00:00:39.953 --> 00:00:43.053
outside, such as the sun or a warm rock.

10
00:00:44.113 --> 00:00:46.533
Ectotherm does not mean a cold animal.

11
00:00:47.367 --> 00:00:49.867
Heat leaves the body by four routes.

12
00:00:51.027 --> 00:00:54.867
Radiation is emission and absorption of electromagnetic energy.

13
00:00:55.847 --> 00:00:58.487
Conduction passes heat through direct contact.

14
00:00:59.547 --> 00:01:02.467
Convection is transfer by moving air or water.

15
00:01:03.467 --> 00:01:06.947
Evaporation carries heat away as water turns to vapour.

16
00:01:07.867 --> 00:01:09.847
The first three run either way.

17
00:01:10.407 --> 00:01:13.907
Evaporation only removes heat, and it costs water.

18
00:01:14.717 --> 00:01:19.257
Plot an endotherm's resting metabolic rate against air temperature.

19
00:01:20.177 --> 00:01:24.937
Inside the thermoneutral zone, the line is flat, at basal metabolic rate.

20
00:01:25.817 --> 00:01:28.517
Below the lower critical temperature, it climbs.

21
00:01:29.517 --> 00:01:34.617
Above the upper critical temperature, it rises again, as panting and sweating take

22
00:01:34.617 --> 00:01:41.417
work. In 1950, Scholander and Irving found that thick-furred arctic mammals stay

23
00:01:41.417 --> 00:01:44.277
flat into much colder air than tropical ones.

24
00:01:45.250 --> 00:01:51.130
The steepness of the cold limb equals thermal conductance, how readily heat leaks

25
00:01:51.130 --> 00:01:52.830
out per degree of difference.

26
00:01:53.770 --> 00:01:55.570
These numbers are invented for practice.

27
00:01:56.470 --> 00:02:03.770
A small endotherm holds 37 °C, with a conductance of 0.020 W per

28
00:02:03.770 --> 00:02:10.170
K. At 27 degrees, the gap is 10 K, so it makes 0.20 W.

29
00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:16.350
At 17 degrees, the gap is 20 K, so it makes 0.40 W.

30
00:02:17.490 --> 00:02:23.010
Fluff the fur until conductance halves, and that cost drops back to 0.20 W.

31
00:02:23.883 --> 00:02:27.523
A set point is the value a control system defends.

32
00:02:28.283 --> 00:02:29.783
In fever, it is raised.

33
00:02:30.383 --> 00:02:34.883
In torpor, a bird or mammal's body temperature drops under control.

34
00:02:35.823 --> 00:02:42.543
In hibernating American black bears, metabolism fell to 25% of basal while body

35
00:02:42.543 --> 00:02:45.943
temperature stayed between 30 and 36 °C.

36
00:02:46.863 --> 00:02:48.723
Cooling alone does not explain that.

37
00:02:49.623 --> 00:02:56.563
Keep one rule. For an endotherm at steady state, heat made equals heat lost, and the

38
00:02:56.563 --> 00:03:00.163
temperature it defends can shift, as in fever or torpor.
